i think it's important to clear up this  question of our comparative economic  performance actually  so we calculate gdp in a different way  to  pretty much every other country  something that the office for national  statistics and the bank of england  have pointed out now if you if you  correct for that difference or look at  it in a more comparable way nominal gdp  what you find is  our performance is actually very much in  line with all our international kids  well actually it's a little bit better  than places like canada italy spain and  germany when i when i looked at it  but it's definitely you know in line to  slightly better and actually our  employment performance if you look at  unemployment or the change in employment  is again better than most of our  international peers so  but is there much to celebrate in you  know 2020s numbers when you look at them  no what what the numbers show is that  our economy suffered a significant shock  last year and whilst there was some  resilience  in winter what's clear is that right now  many families  many businesses are experiencing  hardship and that's why it's right that  we've provided  the comprehensive support that we have  through our plan for jobs  and we'll be setting up the next stage  of that at budget in early march  and when you look back at the kind of  interventions you made last year you  know is there anything that you think  well we could have done it a little bit  differently to have more of an impact to  get the economy going a bit more  well i think you could always  second-guess yourself but i think it's  important that we  respond to the situation as we find it  of course we're making decisions  in the face of uncertainty but i think  you know i think but you know what i  mean  any regret regrets that that might feed  into your decision-making now that  you're coming out towards  i think i think the key thing is to make  sure that we have a sense of priorities  and  my priority has always been to try and  protect as many people's jobs  as possible that was the priority at the  start it remains my priority now and i  think that is the right priority to have  as we think about the next phase  of of the pandemic and how our economy  recovers through it  and you think that you've got that  balance right do you well i mean people  leave that for other people to judge but  with interventions like the furlough  scheme  you know which we were able to put  together a short period of time  and our support for those who are  self-employed all of that has helped  protect millions of people's jobs and we  know from the data  that that has helped unemployment you  know whilst it is obviously rising and  many people have lost their jobs  is lower than many people forecast in  part because of the interventions that  we've put in place  i think most people have acknowledged  that that support has  lowered unemployment relative to what it  otherwise would have been limited the  number of business failures  you know but of course many people are  suffering hardship haven't lost sight of  that and we will keep providing that  support  and and looking you know the states of  economy a lot of businesses a lot of  households are just wondering  you know is life ever going to return  to normal so what's your message to them  of course understands people's  anxiety and frustration about everything  but i think what  people would want is for this to  hopefully be the last knock down  and we want to make it feel good about  that i think what i do feel very good  about is the vaccine roll out and i pay  enormous tribute to everyone who's  been involved with that huge national  effort and that gives us reasons for  cautious optimism  so what the prime minister and i are now  doing and i think what people want us to  do is  is to be careful and cautious look at  all the data that we're getting in  base our decisions on that evidence and  the prime minister will set out that  road map  of the week of february the 22nd but do  you understand that the frustration i  think that a lot of businesses and  households are failing because you know  they want to  plan they want to know what to do with  their lives and at the moment a lot of  people just feel like they're in limbo  yeah i  absolutely understand that frustration  it is a time of uncertainty but what i  would tell people is certainly on the  economic side  you know our support runs all the way  through to spring furlough for example  to the end of april  and we'll have a budget early in march  to set out the next phase of economic  support  to provide people with that reassurance  and certainty and that will follow  from the prime minister's roadmap the  week before and i know that you're not  going to give away the details of what's  in that budget but can you just give us  a sense of  you know what the broads theme is going  to be well  as as we've been discussing my priority  throughout this crisis has always been  to try and protect as many people's jobs  as possible through this very difficult  period that will remain my overriding  economic  priority it's not about kind of raising  taxes it's not about trying to balance  the books right now well i think  public finances are important and we've  only been able to provide the support  that we have  because of a strong economy and strong  public finances coming into this  and i want to make sure that whenever  the next  shock hits the country that we can also  respond in the same way  that will require our public finances to  be put back in a strong and sustainable  position and i've said i will always be  honest  and upfront with people about what  exactly that means
 

